# CsvConvert — CSV → Markdown / JSON for AI-agent handoff

A single PowerShell 7 script with a full-screen text UI for Windows Terminal. Browse to a
`.csv` / `.tsv` file, choose Markdown and/or JSON, convert, check the preview, repeat.
No modules, no installs, no admin rights. The terminal is restored exactly as it was on exit.

## Run

```powershell
pwsh -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "C:\path\to\CsvConvert\app\CsvConvert.ps1"
```

From an already-open PowerShell 7 prompt, `.\CsvConvert.ps1` works too. Other forms:

```powershell
.\CsvConvert.ps1 -Path "C:\Data\inventory.csv"                 # open this file straight away
.\CsvConvert.ps1 -InputDir "D:\exports" -OutputDir "D:\agent"  # one-off folder override
```

## Requirements

- Windows 11, PowerShell 7.x (`pwsh`), Windows Terminal (the legacy console works but is less tested)
- Nothing else: no modules, no Excel, no admin rights, no changes to the system

## Folder layout

```
CsvConvert\
├── app\
│   ├── CsvConvert.ps1   ← the tool
│   └── README.md        ← this file
├── input\               ← drop CSV files here (the file browser starts here)
└── output\              ← .md / .json files land here
```

The `input` and `output` folders are placeholders and are created automatically if missing.
When you decide on permanent locations, edit the `$Settings` block at the top of the script:

```powershell
$Settings = @{
    InputDir     = '..\input'     # relative paths resolve from the app\ folder
    OutputDir    = '..\output'
    PreviewLines = 12
}
```

Any of these work: `'C:\Users\jeff\Documents\CsvConvert\input'`, `'%USERPROFILE%\Downloads'`,
`'\\server\share\agent-handoff'`, a OneDrive folder. The three folders do not need to be
siblings — put the script, inputs and outputs wherever suits you. `-InputDir` / `-OutputDir`
on the command line override the settings for a single run.

## Keys

| Screen      | Keys                                                                                  |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| File browser | `↑` `↓` `PgUp` `PgDn` `Home` `End` move · `Enter`/`→` open folder or select file · `Backspace`/`←` parent folder · `P` type or paste a path (folder or file) · `R` refresh · `Q`/`Esc` quit |
| Options     | `↑` `↓` select · `←` `→` `Space` change value · `Enter` choose · `C` convert · `B`/`Esc` back · `Q` quit |
| Result      | `Enter`/`Esc`/`B` back to files · `Q` quit                                               |
| Anywhere    | `Ctrl+C` quits cleanly                                                                |

Going up past a drive root shows the list of drives. The browser lists only sub-folders and
`.csv` / `.tsv` files; hidden and system items are skipped. Options are remembered for the
next file within a session; the browser returns to the same folder with the converted file
selected, so converting several files in a row is quick.

## Options

| Option         | Values                                 | Notes                                                                                                   |
| -------------- | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Output format  | Markdown · JSON · Both                 | Output name = source name with `.md` / `.json`                                                           |
| JSON layout    | Pretty · Compact                       | Compact is one line, roughly 30 % fewer bytes/tokens                                                     |
| Input encoding | Auto-detect · UTF-8 · Windows-1252     | Auto: a BOM or strictly valid UTF-8 ⇒ UTF-8, otherwise Windows-1252 (Excel's plain "CSV" export)         |
| Delimiter      | Auto-detect · Comma · Semicolon · Tab  | Auto: whichever of `,` `;` TAB appears most in the header line (ties → comma)                            |

The result screen shows what was detected ("Read as: Windows-1252 (detected), delimiter comma
(detected)") so a wrong guess is visible before the file goes anywhere. If an output file
already exists you are asked before it is overwritten.

## What the outputs look like

Both formats follow the same rules as the stand-alone `Convert-CsvToMarkdown.ps1` /
`Convert-CsvToJson.ps1` scripts:

- UTF-8 without BOM; non-ASCII text (José, Zürich, 中文) is written as-is, never `\uXXXX`.
- Rows where every cell is empty are dropped (Excel's trailing `,,,` lines).
- **Markdown**: one GFM table. Only `|` is escaped (`\|`); embedded line breaks become a single
  space; backslashes are left alone so `C:\Data\x.csv` reads correctly in the raw file.
- **JSON**: an array of objects, keys in header order, always an array even for one row.
  All values are strings (no type inference — `"007"` stays `"007"`). An empty cell is `""`;
  a cell missing from a short row is `null`.

## Caveats

- Duplicate column headers make `Import-Csv` fail ("The member X is already present") — the
  error is shown, nothing is written. Blank headers become `H1`, `H2`, … with a warning shown
  on the result screen.
- `Import-Csv` strips leading whitespace from unquoted cells and collapses trailing whitespace
  to one space; quoted cells keep theirs. Excel does not quote space-padded cells.
- Rows with more fields than the header lose the extras; shorter rows get empty/`null` cells.
- A header-only or empty file is rejected ("CSV contains no data rows").
- Two source files with the same name in different folders map to the same output name — the
  overwrite prompt will tell you.

## Troubleshooting

- **"running scripts is disabled on this system"** — use the run line above; `-ExecutionPolicy Bypass`
  applies to that one process only and needs no admin rights. If the file was downloaded, you
  can instead run `Unblock-File .\CsvConvert.ps1` once.
- **"cannot be run because it contained a #requires statement for PowerShell 7.0"** — it was
  started with Windows PowerShell 5.1 (e.g. right-click → *Run with PowerShell*). Use `pwsh`.
- **`pwsh` is not recognized** — PowerShell 7 is not on the PATH for this prompt; use the full
  path, usually `"C:\Program Files\PowerShell\7\pwsh.exe"`.
- **Accented characters look wrong in the preview** — the file was most likely Windows-1252 and
  auto-detect chose UTF-8 (possible only for files that happen to be valid UTF-8); set
  *Input encoding* explicitly and convert again.
- **Terminal looks odd after a crash** — the script restores the screen even on errors, but if
  pwsh itself was killed, close the tab or run `cls`.

## Before handing files to an agent

Review the output for confidential data first. The `output` folder is the review point:
nothing leaves your machine unless you copy it somewhere.

